```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
```

`reverse_geocode()` answers one question — what is near this point — but the
database behind it holds every civic address in the country and is open to any
query you care to write. `nar_connection()` returns a plain DBI connection, so
the whole dplyr vocabulary works against it, evaluated inside DuckDB rather
than in R.

This vignette assumes you have already worked through
`vignette("cangeocode")`, in particular the `NAR_CACHE_PATH` setup and the
one-time import.

```{r connect, message = FALSE}
library(cangeocode)
library(dplyr)

con <- nar_connection()
```

## The tables

```{r tables}
DBI::dbListTables(con)
```

Two of those are NAR's addresses and locations; `MunAlias`, `PostalMun` and
`Streets` are gazetteers `normalize_address()` builds at import. The rest are
companion sources, present only if their own import has been run and kept beside
NAR rather than merged into it: `RqaAddresses` and `RqaStreets` are Quebec's own
register (`rqa_import()`), and `RnfSegments` and `RnfStreets` are Statistics
Canada's road network file (`rnf_import()`), which carries street centrelines
with an address range on each side rather than addresses.
`vignette("geocoding")` covers what each is for.

**`Addresses`** has one row per civic address — the street name and number, the
mailing address, the municipality, the postal code, and the point geometry.

```{r addresses-cols}
tbl(con, "Addresses") |> colnames()
```

**`Locations`** has one row per *location*, which is what NAR calls a distinct
site. Several addresses can share one (an apartment building is the obvious
case). It carries the census and electoral geography that `Addresses` does not:
census subdivision code, federal electoral district, economic region.

```{r locations-cols}
tbl(con, "Locations") |> colnames()
```

`LOC_GUID` joins the two, and `ADDR_GUID` is the address key.

```{r scale}
tbl(con, "Addresses") |> count() |> collect()
```

Column names are kept exactly as StatCan ships them, in
`SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`, so the NAR User Guide reads directly onto what you see
here.

## Filtering

Anything dplyr can express becomes SQL. Nothing is fetched until you collect.

```{r filter}
kingsway <- tbl(con, "Addresses") |>
  filter(MAIL_MUN_NAME == "VANCOUVER", MAIL_STREET_NAME == "KINGSWAY")

kingsway |> count() |> collect()
```

Aggregation runs in the database too, over all 17 million rows, and stays fast
because DuckDB scans columns rather than rows.

```{r aggregate}
tbl(con, "Addresses") |>
  count(PROV_CODE, sort = TRUE) |>
  head(5) |>
  collect()
```

You can inspect the generated SQL at any point with `show_query()`, which is
often the quickest way to see whether a verb translated the way you expected.

```{r show-query}
kingsway |> count() |> show_query()
```

## Joining to `Locations`

Join when you need the geography that only `Locations` carries.

```{r join}
tbl(con, "Addresses") |>
  filter(MAIL_MUN_NAME == "VANCOUVER", MAIL_STREET_NAME == "KINGSWAY") |>
  left_join(tbl(con, "Locations") |> select(LOC_GUID, FED_ENG_NAME, ER_ENG_NAME),
            by = "LOC_GUID") |>
  count(FED_ENG_NAME, sort = TRUE) |>
  collect()
```

## Getting the geometry out: `collect_nar()`

`collect()` brings back a plain data frame, and the `geom` column arrives as
something unusable — DuckDB geometry has no R representation. Use
`collect_nar()` instead. It transfers the geometry as WKB, reads the storage
CRS from the database rather than assuming one, and hands you an `sf` object.

```{r collect-nar}
addresses <- tbl(con, "Addresses") |>
  filter(MAIL_MUN_NAME == "VANCOUVER", MAIL_STREET_NAME == "KINGSWAY") |>
  select(ADDR_GUID, CIVIC_NO, MAIL_STREET_NAME, MAIL_POSTAL_CODE,
         geom_source, geom) |>
  collect_nar()

addresses
```

```{r crs}
sf::st_crs(addresses)$input
```

EPSG:3347 is Statistics Canada Lambert, a projected CRS in metres — which is
why distances from `reverse_geocode()` need no conversion.

Pass `crs` to reproject on the way out. This happens inside DuckDB, and the
argument accepts a bare EPSG number, an authority string, or an `sf::st_crs()`
object.

```{r reproject}
tbl(con, "Addresses") |>
  filter(MAIL_MUN_NAME == "VANCOUVER", MAIL_STREET_NAME == "KINGSWAY") |>
  select(ADDR_GUID, geom) |>
  head(3) |>
  collect_nar(crs = 4326)
```

`collect_nar()` needs a lazy table still attached to its connection — it looks
the connection up to read the CRS. Handed a data frame you have already
collected, it says so rather than failing somewhere deep in dbplyr.

```{r collected-error, error = TRUE}
already_collected <- tbl(con, "Addresses") |> head(1) |> collect()
collect_nar(already_collected)
```

### Addresses with no geometry

About 65,000 addresses have neither a building nor a blockface point.
`collect_nar()` maps their `NULL` geometry to an **empty point** rather than
dropping the row, so counts stay honest. Filter them out with
`sf::st_is_empty()` before anything that expects real coordinates.

```{r empty-geometry}
tbl(con, "Addresses") |>
  filter(is.na(geom_source)) |>
  select(ADDR_GUID, MAIL_MUN_NAME, geom) |>
  head(3) |>
  collect_nar() |>
  mutate(empty = sf::st_is_empty(geom))
```

## A note on internal columns

`Addresses` and `Locations` both carry `x` and `y` columns. They duplicate
`geom` and exist only to let DuckDB skip whole blocks of rows during a radius
query — the single largest performance win in the package. They are **dropped
at the collection boundary** by both `collect_nar()` and `reverse_geocode()`,
because a reprojected result would leave them silently stale. Treat them as
package internals rather than data.

## Read-only connections

The connection is opened read-only, so the database cannot be modified by
accident and several R sessions can query the same file at once. Writing to it
is an error:

```{r read-only, error = TRUE}
DBI::dbExecute(con, "CREATE TABLE scratch (id INTEGER)")
```

Materialise anything you want to keep in R, or in a database of your own.

```{r disconnect}
DBI::dbDisconnect(con)
```
